{
  "id": "prime-lag-autokey-openings",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Prime-lag autokey against fixed opening cribs",
  "question": "Do the declared prime-lag CT/PT autokey rules with one-rune primers recover any of nine fixed 12-rune page-start openings?",
  "reader_question": "Do any prime-delayed autokey settings produce one of the nine proposed openings?",
  "summary": "No exact opening matches occur. The 638 settings give 315 distinct 12-rune prefix predictions on 53 pages and 316 on 2 pages; longer lags do not reach feedback inside those prefixes.",
  "reader_finding": "None of the tested settings produces any of the nine proposed openings on these page starts. Longer known-text controls do recover correctly.",
  "reader_limit": "Only the fixed one-rune primers, delays and nine opening guesses were tested. Longer primers or different plaintexts could behave differently.",
  "finding": "Across 35,090 nonempty page/settings and 315,810 exact opening comparisons, zero matches occur. All 638 full 64-rune plant recovers under both dense recurrence and independent direct/closed-form checks. Distinct prefixes/page are 315 on 53 pages and 316 on 2 pages; the 348 lag>=13 states reduce to 29 uniform-shift prefixes.",
  "outcome": "not-supported",
  "limitations": "A bounded exact-opening compatibility test, not a general autokey exclusion. Six lag values 13..31 do not reach feedback within 12 target runes, so parameter labels overcount distinct predictions. Single-rune primers repeat until lag; arbitrary primers are untested. Source dense-rule reimplementation is not original C++ runtime parity.",
  "next_steps": "A longer independently specified plaintext continuation can exercise the larger delays; arbitrary lag-length primers require a separately bounded key rule and matched power controls.",
